wlcs v1.8.1: place_above_simple and place_below_simple assert that nothing takes the pointer focus.

Both tests put two overlapping subsurfaces under the pointer, restack them with `wl_subsurface.place_above` or
`place_below`, and then check the surface under the pointer. The first assertion (the surface that was moved down, or
the one that was covered, is not the focus) is the check that the restack happened. The second then demands that the
other subsurface is not the focus either, which no compositor that sends pointer focus to a subsurface can satisfy,
and every other test in the file (`subsurface_gets_pointer_input`, `pointer_input_correctly_offset_for_subsurface`,
`subsurface_moves_under_input_device_*`) demands that it does. The tests fail the same way on Mir
(canonical/wlcs#396). The second assertion is turned round: the surface that is on top after the restack is the focus.

Applied by scripts/wlcs.sh with `git apply`; drop it when wlcs fixes the assertion upstream.

diff --git a/tests/subsurfaces.cpp b/tests/subsurfaces.cpp
index 2343e11..a112b7e 100644
--- a/tests/subsurfaces.cpp
+++ b/tests/subsurfaces.cpp
@@ -395,7 +395,9 @@ TEST_P(SubsurfaceTest, place_below_simple)
     ASSERT_THAT(input_device->focused_window(), Ne((wl_surface*)subsurface_moving_down))
         << "subsurface.place_below() did not have an effect";
 
-    ASSERT_THAT(input_device->focused_window(), Ne((wl_surface*)subsurface))
+    // The sibling that was not moved is now the topmost surface under the pointer, and pointer focus goes to the
+    // surface under the pointer, a subsurface included, as every other test in this file expects.
+    ASSERT_THAT(input_device->focused_window(), Eq((wl_surface*)subsurface))
         << "wrong surface/subsurface on top";
 }
 
@@ -413,7 +415,8 @@ TEST_P(SubsurfaceTest, place_above_simple)
     ASSERT_THAT(input_device->focused_window(), Ne((wl_surface*)subsurface_being_covered))
         << "subsurface.place_above() did not have an effect";
 
-    ASSERT_THAT(input_device->focused_window(), Ne((wl_surface*)subsurface))
+    // The surface that was placed above its sibling is the topmost one under the pointer and takes the focus.
+    ASSERT_THAT(input_device->focused_window(), Eq((wl_surface*)subsurface))
         << "wrong surface/subsurface on top";
 }
 
